<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bob Marley</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1945">1945</a>–<a href="/years/1981">1981</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Robert Nesta Marley</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Bob Marley was a Jamaican singer and songwriter who brought reggae to a worldwide audience with his band the Wailers. A follower of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rastafari">Rastafari</a>, he wrote songs of spiritual faith, protest and love, such as No Woman, No Cry, Get Up, Stand Up and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/redemption-song">Redemption Song</a>. He remains <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jamaica">Jamaica</a>'s best-known cultural figure; he died of cancer in 1981 aged 36.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bob-marley.jpg" alt="Bob Marley" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Not credited · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bob_Marley_circa_1976_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Robert Nesta Marley was born on 6 February 1945 at the farm of his maternal grandfather in Nine Mile, in Saint Ann Parish, then the Colony of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jamaica">Jamaica</a>. His father, Norval Sinclair Marley, was a white Englishman who supervised a subdivision of land for war veteran housing. His mother, Cedella Malcolm, was an Afro-Jamaican aged 18 when they married. Norval gave little financial support and died when Marley was 10. Marley began to play music with Neville Livingston, later known as Bunny Wailer, at school in Nine Mile.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1963 Marley, Bunny Wailer, Peter Tosh and others formed a group called the Teenagers, which became the Wailing Rudeboys, the Wailing Wailers and finally the Wailers. Their single Simmer Down for Coxsone Dodd's label became a Jamaican number one in February 1964, and they recorded regularly at Studio One. By 1966 the core of the group was the trio of Marley, Wailer and Tosh.</p><p>In 1972 Chris Blackwell of Island Records offered the Wailers an advance of £4,000 to record an album, seeing in Marley the rebel image needed to reach a rock audience. They recorded Catch a Fire at Harry J's studio in Kingston, and with Burnin', both 1973, it brought them international attention. Marley then carried on as Bob Marley and the Wailers, with the I Threes, Judy Mowatt, Marcia Griffiths and his wife Rita, as backing singers. A live version of No Woman, No Cry was his first hit outside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jamaica">Jamaica</a>, in 1975.</p><p>Marley survived an assassination attempt at his home in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jamaica">Jamaica</a> in 1976, believed to have been politically motivated. He left Jamaica at the end of that year and spent two years in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, where he recorded Exodus. His album Survival in 1979 reflected his support for African struggles, and in April 1980 he was invited to perform at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zimbabwe">Zimbabwe</a>'s independence celebration.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In July 1977 Marley was diagnosed with acral lentiginous melanoma, a skin cancer beneath the nail of his right big toe. He declined to have the toe amputated, citing religious beliefs, and continued to tour. A longtime follower of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rastafari">Rastafari</a>, he was baptised into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopian-orthodox-church">Ethiopian Orthodox Church</a> on 4 November 1980 and died on 11 May 1981. He received a state funeral in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jamaica">Jamaica</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Bob Marley” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…African historical scholarship, among them Diop and the Nigerian historian Kenneth Onwuka Dike, and its cultural side flourished in the Négritude poets, the First World Festival of Negro Arts in Dakar in 1966, the festival known as FESTAC in Lagos in 1977 and the reggae of <b>Bob Marley</b>.</blockquote><a href="/movement/pan-africanism">Pan-Africanism · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>&quot;Redemption Song&quot; is the last track of Uprising, the twelfth album of <b>Bob Marley</b> and the Wailers, produced by Chris Blackwell and released by Island Records in 1980.</blockquote><a href="/songs/redemption-song">Redemption Song</a></li><li><blockquote>Rastafari and reggae, led by <b>Bob Marley</b>, made Jamaican culture globally influential.</blockquote><a href="/state/jamaica">Jamaica</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Nine Mile, in what is now <a href="/countries/jamaica">Jamaica</a>. Died in Jackson Memorial Hospital, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-states">the United States</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=18.3,-77.28&amp;year=1968" aria-label="Nine Mile on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2455 1485 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Bob Marley"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M2905 1755h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M2905 1755h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=18.3,-77.28&amp;year=1968">What was here in 1968? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
